//? Title: Ramer-Douglas-Peucker: actual finite-segment splits and retained vertices
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A densely sampled polyline often uses more vertices than its geometry requires.
Ramer-Douglas-Peucker retains original endpoints and splits only where an
intermediate vertex lies beyond a distance tolerance from the finite replacement
segment. The actual iterative native split history now supports the method
diagram. The original201-point wiggly curve remains, and an overshooting
collinear control exposes why infinite-line distance was insufficient.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

For a chord from a to b, project p onto its direction and clamp the projection
parameter to the finite segment:
$$ t=\operatorname{clamp}_{[0,1]}\frac{(p-a)\cdot(b-a)}{\|b-a\|^2},\qquad d(p,[a,b])=\|p-a-t(b-a)\|. $$
\alttext(Clamp a projection to the finite segment before measuring its distance.)
Coincident endpoints use ordinary point distance. The implementation evaluates
equivalent normalized-direction arithmetic in extended precision to avoid
squared double overflow. If the first farthest interior vertex exceeds epsilon,
retain it and process both child ranges; otherwise replace that range by its
chord.

The epsilon-neighborhood of a segment is convex. When all original vertices of
an accepted range lie in it, every original connecting edge does too. Thus the
resulting retained chords give a directed original-to-simplified distance bound
(up to arithmetic roundoff). This does not prove minimum vertex count, topology
preservation or that every retained farthest vertex is necessary in all possible
simplifications. Worst-case distance work is quadratic; an iterative
pending-range stack uses linear working storage.

